steps.message:
Now let me create the PoC. The fuzzer format is:
- Byte 0: `prefixedChoice` (0 = no prefix, 1 = prefix with `:user `)
- Bytes 1+: IRC data split on `\r\n`, each line gets `\n` appended

If `prefixedChoice` is 0 (false), the line is sent as-is with `\n` appended.
If `prefixedChoice` is non-zero (true), the line becomes `:user <line>\n`.

For the PRIVMSG case with a prefix, the emitted line to `irc_parse_incoming_line` would be `:user PRIVMSG target :\001\n`. The prefix parser extracts `nick=user`, then the remaining line `PRIVMSG target :\001` goes to `irc_server_event`.

Wait — actually let me also look at `event_get_params` for the message extraction more carefully. When `event_get_params(data, 2, &target, &msg)` is called with `data = "target :\001"`, the second parameter extraction with count=0 (last param) will use `strip_params_colon` (only with `PARAM_FLAG_GETREST`). Actually no — looking at the code:

```c
char *event_get_params(const char *data, int count, ...)
{
    ...
    rest = count & PARAM_FLAG_GETREST;
    count = PARAM_WITHOUT_FLAGS(count);

    while (count-- > 0) {
        str = (char **) va_arg(args, char **);
        if (count == 0 && rest) {
            strip_params_colon(datad);
            tmp = datad;
        } else {
            tmp = event_get_param(&datad);
        }
        if (str != NULL) *str = tmp;
    }
```

With `count=2` and no `PARAM_FLAG_GETREST`, it calls `event_get_param` for both. The second `event_get_param` call: if the data starts with `:`, it returns `data+1` and moves past. So for `data = "target :\001"`, first call extracts `target`, second call gets `:\001` → returns `\001` (after the colon). So `msg = "\001"` (the single byte 0x01).

